dialect protocol
protocol stdio.pdf.1.7.snapshot
version 1
schema stdio.pdf.1.7
start header

# `stdio.pdf.1.7` pack payload is the structural snapshot model: length-prefixed schema/version,
# pages, info, indirect-object graph, and trailer. The shared
# SEMIO envelope is unwrapped before this protocol is walked.
#
# Per this ticket's own binding instruction (mirroring M2's ZIP/PDF-1.7 exclusion, `📖️grammar-
# recipe.md` §6): model the container framing the real decoder actually reads directly -- the
# `%PDF-` header magic, and the xref/trailer region located via the SAME backward-scan capability
# M2 built for zip -- but do NOT attempt full random-access indirect-object-graph resolution
# (xref `/Prev` chains, hybrid streams, arbitrary backward jumps to resolve any object) in the
# protocol walker; that stays Rust-side, as it already is (`decode_pdf`'s own `Resolver`/
# `build_xref`/`resolve_compressed`, ⚙️engine/component.rs:671-796).
#
# `framing record` (NOT `framing magic ...`) is deliberate, confirmed live by a real
# `walk_protocol` run, not assumed: `Framing::Magic` unconditionally reads/compares EXACTLY 8 raw
# bytes regardless of how many hex digits the declared literal has (`🗣️dsl/📖️grammar/
# 🦀️component.rs`'s own `magic_bytes(value: u64) -> [u8; 8]`) -- PDF 1.7's real magic is only 5
# bytes (`%PDF-`), so `framing magic 0x255044462D` gets read as an 8-byte, left-zero-padded value
# and mismatches the real on-disk bytes (`protocol-framing-magic-fixed-8-bytes`, first hit by
# gif87a's own 6-byte signature). Modeled honestly, same fix gif87a's own protocol file
# documents: a real, individually-read `field magic fixed 5` inside a `header` block -- genuinely
# walked but NOT byte-validated at the PROTOCOL layer (the real Rust `decode_pdf` already
# validates it byte-for-byte, `data.len() < 5 || &data[0..5] != b"%PDF-"`,
# ⚙️engine/component.rs:1236). The version digits that follow (`1.7`) are variable-length ASCII
# text terminated by CR/LF -- this dialect's `Prim` family has no "read decimal ASCII digits to a
# delimiter" primitive (only fixed-width binary types and length-prefixed/greedy byte spans), so
# the version text is NOT separately fielded here; it is part of the SAME opaque region every
# real byte past the header magic falls into, until the backward-scan anchor below.
framing record

chain payload bytes

# Real Rust `decode_pdf` locates its xref/trailer anchor via `find_last_subslice(data,
# b"startxref")` (⚙️engine/component.rs:1242) -- a genuine BACKWARD scan from EOF, because (same
# "trailer describes the middle" family as ZIP's EOCD, W0's own load-bearing finding) nothing
# before that keyword announces where the trailer/xref region begins. `backward`'s own `magic`
# literal is ALSO u64-parsed (`trim_be_bytes(parse_u64_literal(...))`,
# `🗣️dsl/📖️grammar/🦀️component.rs`) -- capped at 8 bytes, same limit `framing magic` hits above --
# so the real 9-byte ASCII keyword `startxref` cannot be declared whole; this file anchors on its
# first 8 bytes, `startxre` (`0x7374617274787265` = `s t a r t x r e`), which is unique enough in
# a well-formed PDF (the only place that byte run occurs) to locate the same real position
# `find_last_subslice` finds, one byte earlier than the literal keyword's own end -- harmless
# since the trailing `f` simply becomes the first byte of the opaque `tail`. The offset digits,
# `xref`/`trailer` dictionary text, and every indirect object preceding this anchor are ASCII/COS
# text with no fixed byte width either (`/Length 123` object-stream lengths, decimal xref
# offsets, `<< ... >>` dictionaries) -- genuinely NOT protocol-Prim-walkable at the byte-fixed-
# field level this dialect provides (STEP/PLY's own cross-dialect-parameterization gap,
# `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §6, is the same family: a grammar-parsed value driving protocol field
# shape). Modeled as one honest opaque tail from the anchor to EOF -- the real per-field COS
# parsing (`Lexer`/`parse_dict_or_stream`/`parse_classic_xref`/`parse_xref_stream`) stays fully
# real and independently tested, Rust-side.
# Once this file declares a `backward` block, `walk_protocol`'s final `pos == bytes.len()` check
# is (correctly, per M2's own documented exception) skipped -- see `protocol_walk_law`, which
# asserts a bounded `consumed`, not `== len`.
